+# RF-02 fixes — library-operations-v4
+
+Status: internal review. Candidate release: ./authoring/server-production/rf02-fixes-v4/release-r1, preview port 4194. Deployment and RF-02-3 completion remain pending. This report supersedes v3 claims about tabletop interaction and normal keyboard coverage.
+
+## Requested behavior and implementation
+
+- Removed every generated chair/cart handle from the shared closing-fixtures GLB and its scene bindings. All three levels retain the same furniture models.
+- Chairs use their existing backs. The cart accepts approach from both ends. Collision-aware walking, runtime torso/arm animation, keyboard pushing and F release use the original surfaces.
+- All six tabletop books are selectable in both variants. Visibility checks omit only the supporting surface; intervening obstacles still block selection. Cart-book coordinates follow the cart in authoritative state; pickup clears the support.
+- RF-02-3 independently develops a randomly selected shelf fault after eight simulation seconds. Furniture work may trigger it earlier. The first book falls immediately; another falls every twelve seconds until repair or a total of three. No countdown or navigation clues were added.
+- STOP now cancels/recoveries furniture actions without calling a missing controller method.
+
+## Verification to date
+
+46/46 original-library and series tests pass. Coverage includes all six tabletop pickup routes in both levels; both cart ends; carried cart-book detachment; absent added handles; autonomous fault timing; all three random shelf branches; recurring/capped falls; repair interruption; full routes with zero/two errors; collision, orientation, rewards, reset and failure freezing. Production build passes with the pre-existing bundle-size warning.
+
+Nine robot/shared-control/camera hashes match the protected baseline. All 17 resources (4,854,347 bytes) match source/build/HTTP on candidate port 4194.
+
+Browser evidence uses the fixed candidate release and the ordinary F handler. RF-02-2 keyboard-only regression picked up the desk book and pushed the cart from the front by 0.909 m. Its complete route restored both chairs and parked the cart: transit uses the supported agent movement API, while furniture pushing and every interaction use normal keyboard events. First-person screenshots show hands at the existing chair back and cart frame. Chair contact errors were 0.007–0.043 m; cart rear contact errors approximately 0.021–0.023 m.
+
+RF-02-3 keyboard diagnosis successfully picked up a desk book and recorded falling/ground transitions for three books without furniture work. Its automated navigation later stalled in the narrow passage beside the cart; this diagnostic is retained as a failure, not represented as a complete keyboard route. The full-route check is separate.
+
+## Reserve
+
+Initial/maximum reserve 100. RF-02-2 movement 1.45 points/m, error 3, chair reward 5, cart reward 8. RF-02-3 movement 1.38 points/m, error 5, chair reward 3, cart reward 5, repair 3, first pickup/return 1 each. Idle/free turn cost zero; blocked movement and repeated actions are charged. Rewards are deduplicated.
